//! stdlib/crypto/csprng.sio — OS-entropy source (genuine CSPRNG input)
//!
//! Reads raw entropy from `/dev/urandom` via the compiler's builtin
//! `read_file`. This is the ONLY randomness source in this repository
//! suitable for private-key material or other security-sensitive use;
//! stdlib/crypto/random.sio is a deterministic PRNG, not this.
//!
//! Measured behaviour worth recording for future maintainers: `read_file`
//! returns a packed, NUL-terminated `string`, and `str_len` on this
//! compiler is a plain byte scan for the first 0x00 (see
//! self-hosted/native/codegen.sio's emit_builtin_str_len) -- it does NOT
//! report the real `read()` byte count. Because /dev/urandom output is
//! uniformly random, roughly 1 byte in 256 is zero, so a single
//! `read_file("/dev/urandom")` call yields a `str_len` that is effectively
//! geometrically distributed (mean ~255 bytes over repeated spikes: 157,
//! 551, 489 were observed), NOT a fixed per-call maximum -- it just means a
//! call occasionally yields fewer bytes than requested. (An earlier version
//! of this comment claimed the scan "does not bias the bytes that ARE
//! returned" -- that claim was FALSE, a conditioning-on-a-stopping-rule
//! fallacy; see the "CORRECTED CLAIM" paragraph below for the actual
//! behaviour and the fix.) `crypto_os_random_bytes` therefore
//! loops, issuing fresh `read_file` calls (each one opens/reads/closes
//! `/dev/urandom` again, so a short read never re-uses truncated data)
//! until `n` bytes have been collected.
//!
//! One further edge case of the same NUL-scan behaviour: `str_len` returns
//! exactly 0 whenever a call's very FIRST byte happens to be 0x00 -- this
//! happens with probability exactly 1/256 (~0.39%) per `read_file` call,
//! independent of `n`, and does NOT indicate anything is actually wrong
//! with /dev/urandom. Treating a single zero-length read as a hard failure
//! would make `crypto_os_random_bytes` spuriously fail on ~1-in-256 calls
//! even when the OS entropy source is perfectly healthy -- unacceptable for
//! a function future TLS key-generation code will call live. So a
//! zero-length read is retried (fresh `read_file` calls are independent;
//! a repeated leading zero byte is not "more of the same" data, it's a new
//! coincidence that has to happen again to persist) rather than treated as
//! fatal. A bounded consecutive-zero-length counter still exists as a
//! genuine circuit-breaker for a truly broken source (permission denied,
//! path missing, or a `read_file` that always returns empty) -- see
//! MAX_CONSECUTIVE_EMPTY_READS below.
//!
//! CORRECTED CLAIM (this module previously stated the opposite here, which
//! was a conditioning-on-a-stopping-rule fallacy -- caught and fixed
//! 2026-08-25): the NUL scan DOES bias the bytes it returns, if you only
//! ever copy the bytes strictly BEFORE the terminating zero. "The scan
//! stopped at position N" is conditioning information -- it happened
//! specifically BECAUSE byte N is 0x00. Every byte in `s[0..N)` is
//! therefore drawn from the sub-population "bytes that precede a zero",
//! which is NOT the same distribution as a uniformly random byte: it can
//! never itself be 0x00. Measured directly: drawing 8192 bytes via the
//! pre-fix `crypto_os_random_bytes` produced ZERO 0x00 bytes, versus an
//! expected ~32 from a genuinely uniform source (P(this by chance) ~=
//! 1.3e-14). The byte at `s[N]` -- one past everything currently copied --
//! IS itself real, freshly-read `/dev/urandom` data (that's precisely why
//! the scan stopped there: it found a real embedded zero), so
//! `crypto_os_random_bytes` below also copies that one extra byte whenever
//! it is safely known to be genuine and there is still room for it -- see
//! `max_trusted_terminator_position()`.
//!
//! Trusting `s[N]` unconditionally would be wrong in one specific case:
//! Madaros's native 1-arg `read_file(path) -> string`
//! (`self-hosted/native/codegen_x86_linux.sio::emit_builtin_read_file`)
//! backs the returned string with a freshly anonymous-mmap'd buffer (>=
//! ~1 MiB, zero-filled by the kernel) and does exactly ONE `read()`
//! syscall into it. If that single `read()` ever returned fewer bytes than
//! requested (a "short read"), everything from the actual end of the real
//! data onward would still be the mmap's own virgin zero-fill -- not
//! `/dev/urandom` output. In that scenario, if `str_len` happened to find
//! no genuine embedded zero within the short-read region, its scan would
//! run off the end of real data and report the *first zero-fill byte* as
//! `N` -- a synthetic zero, not entropy. Appending that byte would inject
//! deterministic (non-random) data into the CSPRNG output, silently.
//! `crypto_os_random_bytes` cannot see the underlying syscall's actual
//! `bytes_read` count (the 1-arg `read_file` API does not expose it), so
//! it cannot distinguish these two cases directly. Instead it uses a
//! probabilistic ceiling: for `N` below `max_trusted_terminator_position()`
//! (64 KiB), the probability that a truly random byte stream of that
//! length contains NO embedded zero is `(255/256)^65536 ~= 1.7e-111` --
//! indistinguishable from impossible -- so any `N` that small is
//! overwhelmingly certain to be a genuine embedded zero, never the
//! zero-fill boundary of an anomalous short read. (Linux itself does not
//! short-read `/dev/urandom` for a single-syscall request of this size
//! absent a delivered signal -- the kernel loops internally until the
//! request is filled -- so this ceiling is not expected to ever bind in
//! practice; it exists purely as defense-in-depth, per this project's
//! "don't blindly trust, verify" discipline.) Measured over 20,000
//! `read_file("/dev/urandom")` calls: max observed `str_len` was small and
//! nowhere near either the 64 KiB ceiling or the underlying ~1 MiB buffer
//! capacity, consistent with every read being a full, single-syscall read
//! (see `docs/superpowers/sdd/2026-08-25-madaros-x25519-plan/final-review-fix-report.md`
//! for the raw measurement). If `N` is ever at or above the ceiling, the
//! extra byte is simply not appended for that call -- functionally
//! identical to the pre-fix behaviour for that one read, and never
//! expected to trigger.
//!
//! RESIDUAL BIAS (found in independent re-review, 2026-08-25; not fixed
//! here, documented for honesty): the fix above closes the catastrophic
//! case -- pre-fix, `crypto_os_random_bytes` was structurally INCAPABLE of
//! ever returning a 0x00 byte at all, at any position, in any draw. It no
//! longer is: the genuine terminator zero byte is now copied whenever it
//! is safely trusted (see above). But a smaller, real, structural bias
//! remains, and this comment previously implied the output was now fully
//! unbiased -- it is not. The `available <= 0` branch is taken whenever a
//! single `read_file("/dev/urandom")` call's very FIRST byte happens to be
//! a genuine 0x00; when that happens, this function does not use that
//! real entropy byte, it discards the whole read and retries with a fresh
//! `read_file` call. Consequence: byte 0 of every `crypto_os_random_bytes`
//! draw can deterministically NEVER be 0x00 -- not merely improbable, but
//! structurally excluded by this retry logic. That deficit then compounds
//! across the rest of a draw, because each subsequent byte's probability
//! of being a *fresh* draw's leading byte (and thus also excluded) is
//! small but nonzero via the same mechanism at internal `read_file`
//! restart points. For a 32-byte draw (the size `crypto_os_random_key32`
//! below actually consumes), the expected fraction of zero bytes across
//! the draw is `1 - (255/256)^31 ~= 0.1143` per byte on average versus the
//! true uniform expectation `1/256 ~= 0.125` -- about 8.6% low. The
//! deficit is worst at the START of a draw and shrinks for longer draws,
//! asymptotically approaching only a ~0.39% deficit (an effective
//! zero-rate of `1/257` instead of `1/256`) for very long draws -- but
//! this module's actual callers only ever draw fixed 32-byte keys, so
//! "the start of the draw" IS the entire key for every real caller today.
//! Independently assessed security impact: small, well under 0.1 bit of
//! Shannon-entropy loss on a 256-bit key -- real, but not remotely
//! catastrophic, and NOT something that needs a code fix right now. Noted
//! here purely so this doc comment stops overclaiming "fixed" as "fully
//! unbiased".
//!
//! The actual root cause of this residual is structural, not a logic
//! error: this module has no way to distinguish "the `read_file` call
//! returned fewer bytes than requested because the OS genuinely gave us
//! less" from "`str_len`'s NUL-scan happened to hit a real embedded zero
//! at some position" -- both look identical from the outside, since
//! `str_len` on this compiler is a NUL-byte scan, not a true byte-count
//! (see the top of this module doc). A proper fix would need a
//! `read_file` variant (or an equivalent Madaros builtin) that exposes the
//! TRUE number of bytes the underlying `read()` syscall actually returned,
//! instead of reconstructing a length by NUL-scanning the result. That is
//! a separate, future compiler/stdlib primitive enhancement -- out of
//! scope for this module to implement on its own.

use net::socket::*

// Bound on CONSECUTIVE zero-length `read_file("/dev/urandom")` results
// before giving up. A single zero-length result is expected background
// noise (see module doc above, ~1/256 per call) and must not fail the
// whole function. Ten in a row from a genuinely healthy source has
// probability (1/256)^10 ~= 1.7e-24 -- indistinguishable from zero -- so
// hitting this bound is strong evidence of a truly broken source (missing
// device file, permission denied, etc.), not bad luck. The counter resets
// to 0 on any read that returns available > 0, since only CONSECUTIVE
// failures are evidence of a broken source.
fn max_consecutive_empty_reads() -> i64 { 10 }

// Ceiling below which a `str_len`-reported NUL position is trusted as a
// genuine embedded `/dev/urandom` zero byte rather than a possible
// anonymous-mmap zero-fill artifact from an anomalous short `read_file`
// syscall -- see the module doc above ("Trusting `s[N]` unconditionally
// ...") for the full derivation. `(255/256)^65536 ~= 1.7e-111`: a truly
// random byte stream this long containing NO zero is not a real
// possibility, so any `N` below this bound is certain (for all practical
// purposes) to be a genuine embedded zero. Measured empirically over
// 20,000 `read_file("/dev/urandom")` calls: max observed str_len was 2527,
// nowhere near this ceiling -- see the fix report referenced above.
fn max_trusted_terminator_position() -> i64 { 65536 }

// Returns (buf, true) with `n` bytes of real OS entropy in `buf[0..n]`,
// or (buf, false) only if `/dev/urandom` produces a zero-length
// `read_file` result max_consecutive_empty_reads() times in a row (a
// genuine circuit-breaker for a broken entropy source -- see module doc).
// A single incidental zero-length read (the ~1/256 leading-zero-byte
// coincidence) is retried, not treated as failure.
pub fn crypto_os_random_bytes(n: i64) -> (RawBuf, bool) with IO {
    let out = rawbuf_new(n + 8)
    var filled: i64 = 0
    var consecutive_empty: i64 = 0
    while filled < n {
        let s = read_file("/dev/urandom")
        let available = str_len(s)
        if available <= 0 {
            consecutive_empty = consecutive_empty + 1
            if consecutive_empty >= max_consecutive_empty_reads() {
                return (out, false)
            }
        } else {
            consecutive_empty = 0
            var to_copy = available
            if filled + to_copy > n {
                to_copy = n - filled
            }
            var i: i64 = 0
            while i < to_copy {
                rawbuf_set(&out, filled + i, str_char_at(s, i) as i64)
                i = i + 1
            }
            filled = filled + to_copy
            // Also copy the genuine terminating zero byte at s[available] --
            // see module doc. Only if the caller's buffer still has room
            // (to_copy was NOT truncated to satisfy `n`, i.e. we consumed
            // the whole `available`-byte prefix) AND `available` is small
            // enough to trust as a real embedded zero rather than a
            // possible short-read zero-fill artifact.
            if to_copy == available && filled < n && available < max_trusted_terminator_position() {
                rawbuf_set(&out, filled, 0)
                filled = filled + 1
            }
        }
    }
    (out, true)
}

// Convenience wrapper: draws 32 bytes of real OS entropy and hands them
// back as a fixed-size array instead of a `RawBuf`, doing the
// `RawBuf` -> `[u8; 32]` extraction loop internally. Every future caller
// (TLS key generation, X25519 private-key material, etc.) needs exactly
// this conversion; centralising it once here avoids re-deriving the same
// small `while i < 32 { arr[i] = rawbuf_get(&buf, i) as u8 }`-shaped
// boilerplate at each call site, where it is easy to get subtly wrong
// (off-by-one, wrong cast, forgetting to check `ok`).
pub fn crypto_os_random_key32() -> ([u8; 32], bool) with IO {
    let (buf, ok) = crypto_os_random_bytes(32)
    var key: [u8; 32] = [0; 32]
    if !ok {
        return (key, false)
    }
    var i: i64 = 0
    while i < 32 {
        key[i as usize] = rawbuf_get(&buf, i) as u8
        i = i + 1
    }
    (key, true)
}
